Your group keeps its meeting notes on a shared laptop. A teammate
has offered authorized storage on another machine. You could copy the
directory there and call it done. But would you recognize the right
copy next month? Could somebody restore from it without changing the
active notes? Rehearse the whole route with two invented documents
first. A copy that arrives is a milestone; a copy that restores is
the point of the exercise.

\begin{objectives}
\item Create a named bundle from a small, known source tree.
\item Rehearse delivery and retrieval locally, then recognize the
additional boundaries of an authorized remote transfer.
\item Restore into a new directory and verify selected paths and bytes
without changing the source.
\end{objectives}

\section{Choose what a successful restore must contain}\label{sec:48-plan}

Our test documents are a meeting note and a contact note with
no real names or addresses. A successful drill will recreate
both files and their expected directory structure. It will
not restore permissions for an entire service, reproduce a
system disk, or establish a permanent backup schedule. For
real documents, agree on ownership, access, retention, and
what to do when somebody who had access leaves the group.
Do not store sensitive files just because someone has a spare
account on a machine. Know who can administer the storage and
whether its backups or snapshots retain a copy after removal.

\input{figures/chapter-48/roundtrip.tikz}

Figure~\ref{fig:48-roundtrip} puts an explicit return path in
the plan. Chapters~\ref{ch:39} and \ref{ch:41} introduced
transfer and recoverable copies. Here you will use both ideas:
what left your machine, what returned to it, and what was
restored are three separate objects. You can do the whole
practice offline. The remote section describes only an optional
step for a host you have confirmed you are authorized to use.

Open a spare window; use \cmd|sh| first if needed for the
POSIX shell syntax. Create a fresh private practice directory.
Stop if the assignment or \cmd|cd| fails. The directories
\texttt{handoff}, \texttt{returned}, and \texttt{restored}
are purposely distinct. None stands in for a directory that
already holds your current documents. The writes below name
new files, but \cmd|>| would truncate them if repeated.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/utp48.XXXXXX") && cd "$lab"
pwd
mkdir ./source ./handoff ./returned ./restored
mkdir ./source/notes
printf '%s\n' 'Practice meeting: start at noon' \
    > ./source/notes/meeting.txt
printf '%s\n' 'Practice contact: use the club noticeboard' \
    > ./source/contact.txt
\end{terminal}

In real use, close editors or obtain a stable source snapshot
approved for the project. A bundle can collect inconsistent
versions if several files are changing while it is made.
Select known directories, inspect the names and types, and
do not include links, special files, or private exports by
accident. Real policy may require preserving metadata or file
types not represented here; document those requirements before
claiming that a backup covers them.

\begin{terminal}
find ./source -type f -print
\end{terminal}

\cmd|find| walks the source tree, \cmd|-type f| selects regular
files, and \cmd|-print| shows paths. Expect exactly the two
document paths, in either order. This particular selection does
not prove that there are no other kinds of entries; before
using a real archive input also inspect the complete tree and
the intended inclusion rules. The practice tree was built with
known plain files, by you, in a newly created directory.

\section{Make one candidate and inventory it}

Our \FirstUse{archive} is a single bundle holding tree entries
and their contents. This one is not compressed, and its
contents are not encrypted at rest. Use the common GNU/BSD
\cmd|tar| options below; this exact command line is not a
promise about every POSIX installation. The tool's manual
for your operating system remains the authority.
\cmd|-c| creates an archive, \cmd|-f| takes the output archive
path as the next argument, and \cmd|./source| is the tree to
include. The archive path is outside the source so it is not
created inside the tree being read. A create operation may
replace an existing archive; do not rerun it over a prior
successful delivery. Here \texttt{round-01.tar} is a new
fixture name.
\IndexCommand{tar}\IndexTerm{backup!restore drill}

\begin{terminal}
tar -cf ./round-01.tar ./source
tar -tf ./round-01.tar
\end{terminal}

Run the second command only after the first succeeds. The
\cmd|-t| operation lists entries instead of extracting
them; \cmd|-f| now names the archive to read. Expect the
source directory, a notes directory and both document entries.
Directory suffixes and ordering may vary. Inspect for missing
or surprising paths. This inventory does not read the restored
files and it is not a license to extract an unknown archive.
Absolute paths, parent-directory references, links, and
special entries need additional scrutiny. For this drill
restore only the archive you created and verified.

\begin{terminal}
cksum < ./round-01.tar
\end{terminal}

\cmd|cksum| reads the bundle on standard input and prints the
checksum and byte count without a filename. Keep the result
if you want to identify accidental damage later. Do not expect
all systems to produce identical archives from the same tree:
format defaults, metadata, order, and other details can vary.
We compare copies of one archive, not two independently created
archives. A checksum may help detect accidental change; it
does not authenticate the source or replace the byte comparison
used below.

\section{Deliver and retrieve without changing the candidate}\label{sec:48-delivery}

The next two local copies simulate delivery to another machine
and retrieval later. They do not simulate network errors,
security, or authorization. They are not independent backups
if all directories live on the same disk. Both target
directories are initially empty, and \cmd|cp -i| asks before
overwriting a file. Decline and inspect if you see a question.
Do not continue with a stale returned file: a previous copy
with the same name may still compare equal even if this
attempt failed.

\begin{terminal}
cp -i ./round-01.tar ./handoff/round-01.tar
cp -i ./handoff/round-01.tar ./returned/round-01.tar
cmp ./round-01.tar ./returned/round-01.tar
printf 'retrieval comparison status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Check each copy succeeded before moving on. The returned archive
comparison should be silent and the status zero. Neither an
error nor a difference meets the condition for extracting this
copy. Keep the original bundle while you investigate. If the
remote side were out of reach when you needed it, this local
archive might matter; one remote copy is not a replacement for
an agreed backup plan.

\begin{dialoguebox}{The return trip}
\Brian{It says the upload finished. Why go get the same file back?}
\Stephen{To check that you can find and retrieve what you sent.}
\Brian{But it's still another copy of the archive. My notes aren't in front of me.}
\Stephen{Right. I skipped a distinction. Retrieval confirms the bundle; extraction tests the restore.}
\Brian{Then the recovered notes go somewhere new, not over my current notes.}
\Stephen{Yes. Compare them before deciding whether anything needs replacing.}
\Brian{So our drill ends with evidence about documents, not just a progress bar.}
\DialogueConclusion{A delivery is not a restore.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\subsection{An optional authorized handoff}

Do not type the following connection example literally.
\texttt{backup.example.invalid} is a placeholder, not a
service. Confirm the intended host and account through your
normal trusted channel, get permission to use a private
destination with enough capacity, and verify the host key
as in Chapter~\ref{ch:38}. If this is someone else's machine,
ask them to provision a fresh directory for this attempt.
Do not bypass changed-host checks, guess credentials, or use
any system whose authorization is unclear. If the remote
setting is not available, finish the offline route instead.

\begin{terminal}
sftp backupuser@backup.example.invalid
\end{terminal}

Start the client from the local practice root, and verify its
local and remote locations after connection. The example
treats \texttt{/approved/new-round-01} as an empty remote
directory already created for this purpose; replace it with
the actual authorized path. If you previously ran the local
practice, choose a separate new local output directory for
this attempt. Use \texttt{remote-return} under the local fixture
root by making it before starting \cmd|sftp|. Stop if it
already exists or the creation fails. Only send the practice
archive if the host owner has approved it.

\begin{terminal}
mkdir ./remote-return
\end{terminal}

Inside the verified \texttt{sftp>} prompt, the following
example shows OpenSSH SFTP subcommands, not shell commands.
\texttt{lpwd} prints the local working directory; \texttt{pwd}
prints the remote one. \texttt{cd} changes remote directory
and \texttt{ls} checks remote entries. Verify the directory
really is the approved new location and no archive exists
there before \texttt{put}; it can overwrite an existing
target. \texttt{put} takes a local source and remote target,
whereas \texttt{get} takes a remote source and local target.
Check each command succeeds before continuing. \texttt{quit}
returns to the local shell. Do not assume a transfer that
was interrupted left no file behind; inspect or coordinate
cleanup before retrying with another fresh destination.

\begin{session}
sftp> lpwd
sftp> pwd
sftp> cd /approved/new-round-01
sftp> pwd
sftp> ls -a
sftp> put ./round-01.tar round-01.tar
sftp> get round-01.tar ./remote-return/round-01.tar
sftp> quit
\end{session}

This sequence is illustrative; no particular server output is
assumed. \texttt{ls -a} includes names beginning with a dot.
A blank listing or one showing only the directory references
is expected for a new directory, depending on the server.
Ask if the remote permissions or visibility are unclear.
Figure~\ref{fig:48-boundary} shows the opposite directions of
the two commands. To verify the downloaded archive from the
local shell, compare it against the original with \cmd|cmp|.
Use the archive that actually came back in the restore
step; nothing on the remote host should extract into your
active documents.
\IndexCommand{sftp}

\input{figures/chapter-48/boundary.tikz}

\begin{quicktable}{p{.30\linewidth}Y}
\toprule
Observation & What to do next \\
\midrule
Upload fails or disconnects & Do not assume no remote file was
created. Check with the host owner before retrying or removing it. \\
Download finishes & Compare against the archive you sent before
extracting any file. \\
Unexpected archive entries & Stop. Verify that you obtained and
inspected the expected trusted archive. \\
Content differs after restore & Keep the current documents and
the evidence; investigate the candidate and the expected source. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Restore into space you can leave unchanged}\label{sec:48-restore}

The offline drill restores the copy in \texttt{returned}; use
the verified \texttt{remote-return} archive path instead only
if doing the authorized variant and it has passed comparison.
Start with an empty \texttt{restored} directory
(\cmd|ls -a| can show hidden entries); never run this over
a previous extraction. Use the \texttt{-t} list first and
stop if any paths are surprising. The parentheses run the
extraction in a subshell, so the parent shell stays in the
fixture root. \cmd|-x| extracts rather than lists; \cmd|-f|
names the file to read. Since the subshell changes into
\texttt{restored}, the archive path starts with two dots and
a slash to reach the parent directory. Check each command
before moving to the next one.

\begin{terminal}
ls -a ./restored
tar -tf ./returned/round-01.tar
(cd ./restored && tar -xf ../returned/round-01.tar)
find ./restored -type f -print
\end{terminal}

Expect two paths under \texttt{./restored/source}, not
two paths replacing the originals. The practice archive
was generated without symlinks or unexpected paths. Do not
extract an unknown archive as a privileged user, and don't
treat this simple list step as a general-purpose inspection
tool for hostile inputs.

\begin{terminal}
cmp ./source/contact.txt ./restored/source/contact.txt
printf 'contact comparison status: %s\n' "$?"
cmp ./source/notes/meeting.txt ./restored/source/notes/meeting.txt
printf 'meeting comparison status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Both comparisons should be silent with status zero. Look at the
paths and the two statuses independently. Reviewing one file
is a useful spot check for a large backup, but cannot prove
that every file has been restored. Here you know how many
files to expect and can check both. In an actual recovery,
compare restored files to a manifest from the time of
backup rather than expecting today's edited originals to
match. Verify that the recovered documents open in the
application where they will be used. Work on the recovered
copies and do not replace the current files without making
and verifying a separate recovery plan.

\input{figures/chapter-48/verification.tikz}

\begin{quicktable}{p{.28\linewidth}Y}
\toprule
Record & Why retain it with the drill notes \\
\midrule
Date and archive name & Identifies which attempt was actually tested. \\
Source selection & Shows which files or tree the archive was
supposed to cover, and what is excluded. \\
Delivery and comparison & Distinguishes a connection attempt from
a returned bundle with verified content. \\
Restore checks & Names the restored paths and whether each of the
intended checks passed. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

The last question in a backup drill is who can repeat it.
Record the restore destination policy and the outcome, not
passwords, complete private paths, or copies of the documents.
If only the person who made the backup can locate or decrypt
it, the group has a coordination problem to solve before a
real outage. A drill may uncover that failure even when every
command worked. Figure~\ref{fig:48-verify} shows the checks
that support a successful conclusion.

\begin{takeaways}
\item Practice the entire return route with nonsensitive data.
\item Compare the retrieved bundle, then verify the restored paths and bytes.
\item Keep the active documents untouched until you know what
you can recover.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{Compare the two transport copies}\label{ex:48-sums}
Compute a checksum and byte count for the local handoff and
the returned archive. They represent different points in
the practice, even though the expected contents are the same.
No new file is needed.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
cksum < ./handoff/round-01.tar
cksum < ./returned/round-01.tar
\end{terminal}
Expect the same two numbers. A checksum here is supporting
evidence, not proof of authorization or integrity against
deliberate tampering.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Detect a wrong restored file}\label{ex:48-wrong}
Compare the original contact note to the restored meeting
note, then to the restored contact note. Show the comparison
status immediately after each. Do not overwrite either file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
cmp ./source/contact.txt ./restored/source/notes/meeting.txt
printf 'wrong-path comparison status: %s\n' "$?"
cmp ./source/contact.txt ./restored/source/contact.txt
printf 'right-path comparison status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}
The first differs and gives status one, the second matches
and gives zero. Even a successful checksum of the archive
does not answer a question about which restored path you used.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Make a short restore record}\label{ex:48-record}
Write a plain-text record of the offline drill with the bundle
name, scope, and result. Check that it says ``offline'' and
does not claim an upload. Remove the record during cleanup.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
If all four practice stages succeeded, a minimal record is:
\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'round-01.tar: offline practice' \
    'Scope: one meeting note and one contact note' \
    'Returned bundle and both restored documents match' \
    > ./drill.txt
cat ./drill.txt
\end{terminal}
If a check failed, record which check and what you stopped
before doing. The record is for people who need to know where
to resume. It is not part of the archive.
\end{solution}

Cleanup: confirm the local fixture root with \cmd|pwd|. Remove
the exact practice files. Omit \texttt{drill.txt} if unused.
If you did the optional remote route, remove only your local
\texttt{remote-return/round-01.tar} and empty directory as
well. Arrange remote cleanup with the authorized host owner;
do not remove somebody else's files or the only copy of a
real backup. This removes scratch data, not live documents.

\begin{terminal}
rm ./restored/source/notes/meeting.txt ./restored/source/contact.txt
rm ./source/notes/meeting.txt ./source/contact.txt
rm ./round-01.tar ./handoff/round-01.tar ./returned/round-01.tar
rm ./drill.txt
rmdir ./restored/source/notes ./restored/source ./restored
rmdir ./source/notes ./source ./handoff ./returned
cd / && rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

